WEBVTT

00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:05.720
Here’s how to run Keep, Change, Try.

00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:07.600
It takes about thirty minutes.

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:12.660
Prepare by putting the team canvas on the wall, or sharing it on screen, if you have one.

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:16.200
If not, that’s fine: the exercise works on its own.

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:20.820
Start with Keep. Ask: “What about this team should we protect?”

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:24.740
Give everyone three minutes to write, then share in a round.

00:00:24.790 --> 00:00:26.740
Start with Keep on purpose.

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:31.320
It reminds people what’s working, and it makes the harder questions easier.

00:00:31.370 --> 00:00:35.640
Then Change. Ask: “What is no longer helping us?”

00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:40.660
Again, three minutes of silent writing, then a round. Listen for patterns.

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:43.840
If several people name the same thing, circle it.

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:47.380
Then Try. This is the most important part.

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:50.960
Ask: “What small experiment will we run next?”

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:53.180
Push for small and concrete.

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:56.000
“Communicate better” is not an experiment.

00:00:56.050 --> 00:01:01.100
“For the next four weeks, we end every meeting by writing down who does what” is.

00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:04.220
Choose one or two experiments. Not more.

00:01:04.270 --> 00:01:08.720
For each one, agree who keeps an eye on it, and when you’ll look at it again.

00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:14.560
Finally, ask the last question on the canvas: “What should make us sit down with the canvas again?”

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:21.980
Typical answers: someone joins or leaves, our purpose or priorities change, or simply every six months.

00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:23.460
Write the answers down.

00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:25.079
That’s your trigger to come back.

